// Load buffering, plain relaxed accesses, under RC11.
//
// Source: Lahav, Vafeiadis, Kang, Hur & Dreyer, "Repairing Sequential
//         Consistency in C/C++11" (PLDI 2017).
//
// RC11 FORBIDS this. Its no-thin-air axiom is acyclic(sb u rf), and the outcome
// needs rf edges (7,3) and (4,6) closing a cycle with the sb edges (3,4) and
// (6,7). Forbidding load buffering outright is the known cost of the repair --
// there is no dependency here to justify anything, so nothing weaker than
// syntactic sb u rf acyclicity rules it out. It is the behaviour MRD exists to
// admit, and LB+deps.lit next door is the case where forbidding it is right.
//
// The assertion used to read `allow` and passed vacuously: until ced8378 the
// solver query was the asserted condition plus the rf equalities and nothing
// else, so r1 and r2 stayed free and any outcome came back satisfiable. With
// execution.ex_p in the query (48026cd) it decided false, and the file was
// parked in litmus-tests-review/ as #57. RC11 was right; the assertion was not.
//
// sMRD, IMM, Power, Grounding, Bridging and Sevcik all allow it -- see
// test6/LB.lit, popl_promising/LB.lit, popl_grounding/LB.lit,
// popl_bridging/LB.lit and sevcik_thesis/LB.lit, which asserts exactly that.
// Under [RC11] MoRDor generates 21 executions, 25 with the no-thin-air check
// stubbed out; the four it removes are the witnesses.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  r1 := x;
  y := 1
} ||| {
  r2 := y;
  x := 1
}
%%
forbid (r1 = 1 && r2 = 1) [RC11]
